* s2gcolor.scheme
* dash translation of Stata's s2gcolor scheme.
*
* Source Stata scheme : /Applications/StataNow/ado/base/s/scheme-s2gcolor.scheme
*                       (Stata #include chain: s2color -> s2gcolor)
* Stata version       : StataNow 19.5 (MP), born 12 Aug 2026
* Extracted (UTC)     : 2026-09-19T16:13:55Z
* Translated          : 2026-09-19
*
* Derived mechanically from test/fixtures/scheme_truth.json, which
* scripts/extract_scheme_truth.py resolves from Stata's own scheme and style
* files.  Do not hand-edit values here: re-run the extractor, then regenerate.
* The values below were true of the Stata version named above; if Stata's
* style files change, this file is stale until the fixture is regenerated.
*
* Every colour is written as a RESOLVED #rrggbb hex triplet, never as a Stata
* colour name.  OptionTranslator._COLOR_MAP knows only 72 names, and
* SchemeParser._color() silently substitutes its #000000 default for any token
* it cannot translate -- the defect that renders whole schemes black.  Hex is
* self-contained and cannot be swallowed that way.
*
* Property categories used by dash SchemeParser:
*   color, symbol, linepattern, linewidth, intensity, gsize, graphsize,
*   symbolsize, clockdir, numstyle
*
* KNOWN GAPS (project rule 2 -- honest omission beats a plausible guess):
*   Stata's s2gcolor also declares 'yesno draw_major_hgrid no' and
*   'yesno draw_major_grid no' (fixture: draw_major_hgrid/draw_major_vgrid).
*   dash's SchemeParser._init_properties() has no reader for either, so they
*   are omitted here rather than written as keys dash cannot honour.
*   The fixture records no null/unresolvable property for s2gcolor.

label "s2gcolor"

* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Background and region colors
* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
color background      #eaf2f3
color plotregion      #ffffff
color legend          #ffffff
color legend_line     #000000
color major_grid      #eaf2f3
color minor_grid      #505050
color heading         #1e2d53
color axisline        #000000

* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Per-plot color cycle (p1-p15)
* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
color p1              #1a476f
color p2              #90353b
color p3              #55752f
color p4              #e37e00
color p5              #6e8e84
color p6              #c10534
color p7              #938dd2
color p8              #cac27e
color p9              #a0522d
color p10             #7b92a8
color p11             #2d6d66
color p12             #9c8847
color p13             #bfa19c
color p14             #ffd200
color p15             #d9e6eb

* Per-plot line color (line/connected plots)
color p1line          #1a476f
color p2line          #90353b
color p3line          #55752f
color p4line          #e37e00
color p5line          #6e8e84
color p6line          #c10534
color p7line          #938dd2
color p8line          #cac27e
color p9line          #a0522d
color p10line         #7b92a8
color p11line         #2d6d66
color p12line         #9c8847
color p13line         #bfa19c
color p14line         #ffd200
color p15line         #d9e6eb

* Per-plot bar/area fill color
color p1bar           #1a476f
color p2bar           #90353b
color p3bar           #55752f
color p4bar           #e37e00
color p5bar           #6e8e84
color p6bar           #c10534
color p7bar           #938dd2
color p8bar           #cac27e
color p9bar           #a0522d
color p10bar          #7b92a8
color p11bar          #2d6d66
color p12bar          #9c8847
color p13bar          #bfa19c
color p14bar          #ffd200
color p15bar          #d9e6eb

* Per-plot marker fill color (scatter)
color p1mark          #1a476f
color p2mark          #90353b
color p3mark          #55752f
color p4mark          #e37e00
color p5mark          #6e8e84
color p6mark          #c10534
color p7mark          #938dd2
color p8mark          #cac27e
color p9mark          #a0522d
color p10mark         #7b92a8
color p11mark         #2d6d66
color p12mark         #9c8847
color p13mark         #bfa19c
color p14mark         #ffd200
color p15mark         #d9e6eb

* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Marker symbol cycle
* Stata s2gcolor supplies these through the bare 'symbol p' class default;
* dash reads 'symbol p<i>' by exact key, so all 15 are spelled out.
* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
symbol p1             circle
symbol p2             circle
symbol p3             circle
symbol p4             circle
symbol p5             circle
symbol p6             circle
symbol p7             circle
symbol p8             circle
symbol p9             circle
symbol p10            circle
symbol p11            circle
symbol p12            circle
symbol p13            circle
symbol p14            circle
symbol p15            circle

* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Line patterns for line/connected plots
* Likewise supplied by Stata through the bare 'linepattern p' class default.
* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
linepattern p1line    solid
linepattern p2line    solid
linepattern p3line    solid
linepattern p4line    solid
linepattern p5line    solid
linepattern p6line    solid
linepattern p7line    solid
linepattern p8line    solid
linepattern p9line    solid
linepattern p10line   solid
linepattern p11line   solid
linepattern p12line   solid
linepattern p13line   solid
linepattern p14line   solid
linepattern p15line   solid

* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Line widths (Stata linewidth style names)
* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
linewidth p           medium
linewidth major_grid  medium

* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Fill intensities (Stata intensity style names)
* 'intensity area' is written from Stata's bare 'intensity' class default
* (no base scheme sets 'intensity area'); dash reads only the keyed form.
* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
intensity p           inten80
intensity bar         full
intensity area        full

* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Font sizes (Stata gsize style names)
* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
gsize text            medium
gsize heading         large
gsize axis_title      medsmall
gsize tick_label      medsmall
gsize note            small

* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Graph size, in inches (consumed by section 10 for figure proportions)
* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
graphsize x           3.12
graphsize y           2.392

* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Default marker size for the p cycle
* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
symbolsize p          medium

* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Legend placement (clock direction) and column count
* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
clockdir legend_position  6
numstyle legend_cols  2

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Grid visibility (Stata `yesno` directives)
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* GRID VISIBILITY.  A Stata grid belongs to an AXIS and the key is named for
* that axis's orientation, not for the direction of the lines:
*   style/axisstyle-horizontal_default.style:18  draw_major_grid <- draw_major_hgrid
*   style/axisstyle-vertical_default.style:18    draw_major_grid <- draw_major_vgrid
* The horizontal axis is x, and its major grid lines are the VERTICAL ones at
* the x ticks; the vertical axis is y, and its lines are HORIZONTAL.
* `yesno draw_major_grid' below is transcribed faithfully from Stata's file but
* is DEAD at scheme level: grep "scheme draw_major_grid" over
* /Applications/StataNow/ado/base/style/ returns nothing.  dash used to read it
* for the y axis, which is why every scheme reported "no grid" and dash drew
* both anyway.
yesno draw_major_hgrid      no
yesno draw_major_vgrid yes
yesno draw_major_grid       no

* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
* HONEST GAP (project rule 2) -- the p#lineplot keys
* -------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Stata does not read a plain `twoway line` plot's line from the
* `p#line` scheme keys.  It reaches the `p#lineplot` keys through two
* style files:
*   style/seriesstyle-p#line.style:5   set line    scheme p#line
*   style/linestyle-p#line.style:4-7   set width   scheme p#lineplot
*                                      set color   scheme p#lineplot
*                                      set pattern scheme p#lineplot
* So `linepattern p#lineplot` is the dash pattern of `twoway line`, while
* the `linepattern p#line` block above is what style/linestyle-p#.style
* reads -- the connecting line of `twoway connected`.  Stata documents the
* three keys in scheme_line_plots.sthlp:29-34 as the colour, thickness and
* pattern "for #th line plot".
*
* NO `color p#lineplot` OR `linepattern p#lineplot` DIRECTIVE IS WRITTEN IN
* THIS FILE, because s2gcolor's #include chain (s2color -> s2gcolor) declares
* none at any level. Of all the scheme files Stata ships, only
* scheme-s2mono.scheme and scheme-s2gmanual.scheme contain a `lineplot`
* directive at all; the three occurrences in scheme-s2color.scheme (lines
* 457, 604 and 1250) are commented-out examples, not settings. There is
* therefore nothing to translate here, and a value written in would be a
* fabrication.
*
* Recorded for the reader, NOT as directives, because these are values Stata
* falls back to rather than values s2gcolor writes
* (scheme_line_plots.sthlp:52-56: "If a scheme does not contain some of the
* line-plot specific entries above, then the look of those elements of line
* plots will be determined by default entries that are shared among all
* plottypes"; the shared entries are `color p#` and `linepattern p`, per
* scheme_shared_plots.sthlp:20-31):
*   linepattern p#lineplot -> linepattern p = solid
*       (/Applications/StataNow/ado/base/s/scheme-s2color.scheme:599)
*   color       p#lineplot -> color p#, i.e. the `color p1`..`color p15`
*       values already written above
*       (/Applications/StataNow/ado/base/s/scheme-s2color.scheme:430-444)
